# The exact stdout `scripts/avm-hot-bench-report` writes for each fixture in its
# `selftest`, one block per case: the report itself (printed once), then the
# annotation set that case selects — none on a pass, the last three lines as
# `::error title=hot-bench::` on an enforced miss, the same three as
# `::warning title=hot-store latency (not enforced on this runner)::` plus the
# `[gap]` line and its number on a hosted runner.
#
# `selftest` diffs its output against the matching block and fails on any
# difference; it does not grep for substrings, so a changed title, a reordered
# line or a dropped `[gap]` is a red selftest. The `enforced` block serves both
# ways of selecting enforcement (`--reference` and `AVM_LATENCY_REFERENCE=1`),
# because the step reads the latter and the two must not diverge.
#
# Edit by hand when the reporting is meant to change. There is deliberately no
# regeneration flag: a script that rewrites the file it is checked against can
# never report a drift.
[pass]
hot-bench: acquire 30 ns (limit 1 us)
hot-bench: fault-in 6.5 ms (limit 15 ms)
hot-bench: ok
[enforced]
hot-bench: acquire 30 ns (limit 1 us)
hot-bench: fault-in 22.4 ms (limit 15 ms)
hot-bench: FAIL fault-in 22.4 ms exceeds 15 ms
hot-bench: 1 of 2 row(s) missed
::error title=hot-bench::hot-bench: fault-in 22.4 ms (limit 15 ms)
::error title=hot-bench::hot-bench: FAIL fault-in 22.4 ms exceeds 15 ms
::error title=hot-bench::hot-bench: 1 of 2 row(s) missed
[hosted]
hot-bench: acquire 30 ns (limit 1 us)
hot-bench: fault-in 22.4 ms (limit 15 ms)
hot-bench: FAIL fault-in 22.4 ms exceeds 15 ms
hot-bench: 1 of 2 row(s) missed
::warning title=hot-store latency (not enforced on this runner)::hot-bench: fault-in 22.4 ms (limit 15 ms)
::warning title=hot-store latency (not enforced on this runner)::hot-bench: FAIL fault-in 22.4 ms exceeds 15 ms
::warning title=hot-store latency (not enforced on this runner)::hot-bench: 1 of 2 row(s) missed
[gap] hot-store latency rows measured, not enforced here (hosted runner; set AVM_LATENCY_REFERENCE=1 on a reference machine):
hot-bench: 1 of 2 row(s) missed
